<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!-- Generated by Pandoc using pretext.lua -->
<section xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="sec-solutions5">
    <title>Measuring equivalents of charge in solution</title>

    <p>
        In medicine, the concentration of some solutions is measured using units
        of equivalents (Eq).
        Equivalents is a way of measuring the amount of charge in a sample of
        chemical.
        Equivalents can be calculated as follows:
    </p>

    <p>
        <me>
            \text{Eq}\ =\ \text{moles}\ \times \lvert \text{charge}\rvert
        </me>
    </p>

    <p>
        So if you have <m>2\ \text{moles}</m> of sulfate ions, then you have:
        <me>
            2\ \times \lvert -2\rvert\ =\ 2\ \times\ 2\ =\
        4\ \text{Eq}\ SO_4^{2-}
        </me>
        We can speak about the equivalents of a single ion or about the
        equivalents from a compound.
        If we are concerned about an ionic compound, then we calculate the
        equivalents of charge for each of the ions and then add them together.
        See the example below:
    </p>

    <assemblage>
        <title>Sample calculation</title>

        <p>
            <term>Problem:</term>Calculate the number of equivalents in <m>2\ \text{moles}</m>
            of potassium sulfate.
        </p>

        <p>
            <term>Solution:</term>
        </p>

        <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Figure out the chemical formula if not provided
                    </p>

                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>K_2SO_4</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Figure out the number of moles and charge for each of
                        the ions
                    </p>

                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Potassium:
                            </p>

                            <ol>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Charge: <m>+1</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Ratio:
                                        <m>\frac{2\ \text{moles}\ K^+}{1\ \text{mole}\ K_2SO_4}</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Sulfate:
                            </p>

                            <ol>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Charge: <m>-2</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Ratio:
                                        <m>\frac{1\ \text{mole}\ SO_4^{2-}}{1\ \text{mole}\ K_2SO_4}</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Figure out the number of moles of each of the ions
                    </p>

                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Potassium:
                                <m>2\ \text{moles}\ K_2SO_4\ \times\ \frac{2\ \text{moles}\ K^+}{1\ \text{mole}\ K_2SO_4}\ =\ 4\ \text{moles}\ K^+</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Sulfate:
                                <m>2\ \text{moles}\ K_2SO_4\ \times\ \frac{1\ \text{moles}\ SO_4^{2-}}{1\ \text{mole}\ K_2SO_4}\ =\ 2\ \text{moles}\ </m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Figure out the number of equivalents of each of the ions
                    </p>

                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Potassium:
                                <m>4\ \text{moles}\ K^+\ \times \lvert +1\rvert\ =\ 4\ \text{Eq}</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Sulfate:
                                <m>2\ \text{moles}\ K^+\ \times \lvert -2\rvert\ =\ 4\ \text{Eq}</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Add the number of equivalents for each ion together.
                    </p>

                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>4\ \text{Eq}\ K^+\ +\ 4\ \text{Eq}\ SO_4^{2-}\ =\ 8\ \text{Eq}</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </li>
            </ol>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
        Equivalents if often used along with the volume of a solution as a
        measure of the concentration of charge in a solution (<m>\text{Eq}/\text{L}</m>).
        This is an important unit of concentration that is used when
        administering fluids or drugs intravenously (IV).
        It's important to be aware of the amount of charges present in the
        bloodstream because imbalances can have disastrous consequences for
        patients.
        Because the ion concentrations in blood are so low, the units that are
        often used are milliequivalents per L (<m>\text{mEq}/\text{L}</m>).
        To determine the concentration of a solution in units of mEq per liter,
        we need to first calculate the number of equivalents, then convert this
        to mEq, and finally divide by the volume, measured in liters.
        See the example below:
    </p>

    <assemblage>
        <title>Sample calculation</title>

        <p>
            <term>Problem:</term> If <m>5.0\ \text{g}</m> of potassium phosphate is dissolved
            into <m>150\ \text{mL}</m> of water, what is the concentration of potassium
            phosphate in <m>\text{mEq}/\text{L}</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            <term>Solution:</term>
        </p>

        <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Figure out the formula for the compound
                    </p>

                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>K_3PO_4</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Convert from grams to moles
                    </p>

                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Determine the molar mass of potassium phosphate
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>3\ \times\ 39.10\
+\ 30.97\ +\ 4\times\ 16.00\ =\ 212.27 \text{g}/\text{mole}</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>5.0\ \text{g}\ \times\ \frac{\text{mole}}{212.27\ \text{g}}=0.02355\ \text{moles}</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Calculate the charge and moles of each ion
                    </p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Potassium: 
                            </p>
                            <ol>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Charge: <m>+1</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Moles: <m>0.02355\ \text{moles}\ K_3PO_4\ \times\ \frac{3\ \text{moles}\ K^+}{1\ \text{mole}\ K_3PO_4}=0.07065\ \text{moles}\ K^+</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Phosphate:
                            </p>
                            <ol>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Charge: <m>-3</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>\text{Moles}: 0.02355\ \text{moles}\ K_3PO_4\ \times\ \frac{1\ \text{mole}\ PO_4^{3-}}{1\ \text{mole}\ K_3PO_4}= 0.02355\ \text{moles}\ PO_4^{3-}</m>
                                                            
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Calculate the Eq of each ion and then add them together
                    </p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Potassium:
                            </p>
                            <ol>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>0.07065\ \text{moles}\times \lvert +1\rvert=0.07065\ \text{Eq}</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Phosphate:
                            </p>
                            <ol>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>0.02355\ \text{moles}\ \times \lvert -3\vert=0.07065\ \text{Eq}</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Sum: <m>0.07065\ \text{Eq}+0.07065\ \text{Eq}=0.1413\ \text{Eq}</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Convert from Eq to mEq
                    </p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>0.1413\ \text{Eq}\times\ \frac{1\ \text{mEq}}{10^{-3}\ \text{Eq}}=141.3\ \text{mEq}</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Convert from mL to L
                    </p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>150\ \text{mL}\times\frac{10^{-3}\ \text{L}}{1\ \text{mL}}=0.15\ \text{L}</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Divide mEq by L
                    </p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\frac{141.2\ \text{mEq}}{0.15\ \text{L}}= 942\ \text{mEq}/\text{L}\xrightarrow{\text{Sig. Figs.}} 940\ \text{mEq}/\text{L}</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </li>
            </ol>
    </assemblage>

    <exercises>
        <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How many Eq of potassium ions do you have if you have <m>0.150\ \text{moles}</m> of <m>KCl</m>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>0.150\ \text{Eq}</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How many Eq of oxide are in <m>2.0\ \text{moles}</m> of <m>K_2O</m>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>4.0\ \text{Eq}</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Calculate the number of mEq of calcium ions in <m>0.050\ \text{moles}</m>
                    of <m>Ca(NO_3)_2</m>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>100\ \text{mEq}</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How many mEq of magnesium chloride in <m>0.077\ \text{L}</m> of <m>0.110\ \text{M}</m>
                    magnesium chloride
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>34\ \text{mEq}</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>0.011\ \text{moles}</m> of sodium carbonate is dissolved in <m>0.28\ \text{L}</m> of
                    water, what is the concentration of the sodium carbonate in
                    <m>\text{mEq}/\text{L}</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>160\ \text{mEq}/\text{L}</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Calculate the concentration of sodium chloride in <m>\text{mEq}/\text{L}</m> if
                    <m>0.15\ \text{g}</m> is dissolved into <m>750\ \text{mL}</m>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>6.8\ \text{mEq}/\text{L}</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>
</section>